{"conditionalSetIds":[],"forecasterId":"demographics-ai-2036:run2:inflation:premortem","informationSetArtifactId":"sha256:08cca0245a93bce7db45557eb32cc51a7dd3ff362b6e70101b4e8becd8d5ae7d","modelForecastIds":[],"modelUse":"none","prediction":{"kind":"binary","positiveOutcomeId":"yes","probability":0.35},"questionHash":"sha256:a11e957a2fbf9e72130705a8815d67b5a6c66c3444a8332ee92cdcb4248068e1","questionId":"demographics-ai-2036-cq2","questionVersion":"1.0.0","rationale":"Wave 1: 14 / 24 / 34. I land at 35, which coincides numerically with P3 and decomposes in the OPPOSITE direction on both legs — that disagreement is the finding, not the agreement.\n\nCONJUNCT A (GDP-weighted OECD-38 core CPI/PCE >=2.50%/yr average 2027-2036): MARGINAL 0.72, against their 0.27 / 0.55 / 0.62. Three corrections, all to the same shared error.\n(1) THE COLD LEG IS SMALLER AND PARTLY HOT. All three gave the AI-exposed bloc a 0.12-0.25 weight running 0.0-1.0%. In core PCE that bloc is dominated by non-market-priced components: portfolio management and investment advice priced as a share of assets under management (an AI-capex equity boom INFLATES this line — and 2A locks a $1.4T commitment stack whose equity leg is the thing being priced); commercial banking imputed through net interest margin, which widens on my CQ4 path; P&C insurance measured as premiums net of claims, rising on climate and replacement-cost claims; and much of \"other professional services\" deflated by cost-based PPI proxies, so an AI-driven fall in labour input cost is booked as lower real OUTPUT, not lower price. The market-priced, genuinely deflatable slice is ~0.08-0.12 of core. Correcting the weight alone adds +0.20 to +0.35 pp to each of their builds.\n(2) SHELTER IS WRONG-SIGNED ON DEMOGRAPHICS. P1 explicitly used \"2A's demographics mechanically suppress household formation in Japan, Korea, Italy and Germany\" to hold shelter down. Aging RAISES households per capita (widowhood, single occupancy) and suppresses turnover, so it raises effective rent pressure per unit of stock; Japan's household count kept rising for roughly two decades past its population peak. With construction labour and copper bid away by the grid build, shelter at 3.4-3.6% is central, not hawkish. At a 0.44 core-CPI weight (P2's own) that is +0.18 to +0.26 pp on his aggregate.\n(3) THE WEIGHTING CONVENTION DECIDES IT, AND THE BRIEF'S OWN LOCK POINTS HOT. P2 found that 0.552 pp of his 2.503% comes from Turkiye, Mexico, Chile, Colombia and Costa Rica at an ~8.5% market-GDP weight, called it a convention question, and stopped. Section 3's accounting basis is constant 2025 USD PPP unless the index specifies otherwise; on PPP those five are ~11-12% of OECD-38 (~$78-82T PPP), and at ~6.5% each they contribute 0.72-0.78 pp, not 0.552. That single reweighting takes his aggregate to 2.67-2.73% before corrections (1) and (2). Rebuilt on US-style core weights with my numbers: shelter 0.44 x 3.5 = 1.54; care/health/personal 0.15 x 3.6 = 0.54; AI-exposed-and-partly-imputed services 0.12 x 2.0 = 0.24; other services 0.08 x 2.8 = 0.22; core goods 0.21 x 0.5 = 0.105. US core ~2.64%. PPP-weighted OECD-38: US 0.40 x 2.64 = 1.056; euro area 0.20 x 2.2 = 0.44; Japan 0.085 x 1.8 = 0.153; UK 0.05 x 2.6 = 0.13; Korea 0.04 x 2.1 = 0.084; Canada 0.03 x 2.3 = 0.069; Australia 0.02 x 2.6 = 0.052; the five high-inflation members 0.115 x 6.5 = 0.748; remainder 0.06 x 2.4 = 0.144. Sum ~2.88%. Front-loading does not even have to carry it: 2027-2030 at ~3.1% plus 2031-2036 at ~2.73% averages 2.88%. The bar is 2.50%. MARGINAL A = 0.72, and the residual 0.28 is mostly the lower-tail premortem (a 2029-30 funding accident plus capex bust dragging shelter and the imputed lines down together), not a measurement quibble.\n\nCONJUNCT B (China headline CPI <1.50%/yr average 2027-2036): MARGINAL 0.42, against their 0.58 / 0.48 / 0.52. This is a TEN-YEAR average of HEADLINE, and <1.50% leaves almost no room: at the observed ~0.9% run rate for four years the remaining six must average under 1.9%. Six reasons they are all too high. (i) P3's PPI sign flip is the strongest single datum in Wave 1 and he discounted his own find to 0.52 — producer prices turned positive in March 2026 after ~37-38 consecutive negative months, and Chinese CPI follows PPI with a lag at the food and goods margin. (ii) The property drag is a STOCK adjustment, not a perpetual flow: 38 months of falling new-home prices and a housing share of household assets down from 67% to 52% is an adjustment in progress with an end, and the modal end is 2029-31 — inside the window, leaving five years on the other side. (iii) Anti-involution capacity consolidation is explicitly a PRICE-RAISING industrial policy; all three cited it and only P3 let it move his number. (iv) 2A's own locked arithmetic flips the labour sign: a 60M population decline with worker/retiree going 4.4 -> 2.8 means service-sector unit labour costs turn up through the 2030s. Japan is the analogue every panelist reached for, but Japan deflated with a chronically STRONG yen and no industrial-policy price floor; China runs a managed-depreciating currency, which is reflationary for headline. (v) Headline over ten years contains at least one hog cycle and one oil shock with near-certainty, each worth 0.1-0.2 pp on the decade mean. (vi) Over ten years the probability that Beijing eventually does the consumption-share pivot the 15th Five-Year Plan gestures at is not small. MARGINAL B = 0.42.\n\nCORRELATION: +0.20, and Wave 1 has the SIGN WRONG. P1 took -0.20, P2 -0.10, P3 +0.05. The export-deflation channel (Chinese deflation suppressing OECD core goods) is real but small — a 3%/yr export price fall at 30% pass-through into a 0.21 weight is -0.19 pp/yr on OECD core, and core goods is the lightest and most tariff-walled leg. The dominant channel is the one P3 named and then nearly zeroed: tariffs and fragmentation SIMULTANEOUSLY raise OECD goods prices and glut China's domestic market with diverted export supply. That mechanism produces precisely the joint cell the question asks for, and it is the regime 2A describes. +0.20.\n\nJOINT = 0.72 x 0.42 + 0.20 x sqrt(0.72 x 0.28 x 0.42 x 0.58) = 0.3024 + 0.20 x 0.2216 = 0.3024 + 0.0443 = 0.347 -> 35.","referenceClassIds":[],"schemaVersion":"forecast-workbench.forecast/v1","sealedAt":"2026-11-04T17:31:57.043Z","seriesId":"published-panel","strongestContraryCase":"Not elicited per question. Panelist falsification signposts: {\"signpost\":\"US core PCE 12-month inflation prints at or below 2.2% for three consecutive monthly BEA releases, AND the euro-area HICP core 12-month rate is at or below 2.0% in the same quarter, with neither driven by a measured recession (OECD-38 harmonized unemployment still under 6%).\",\"by_when\":\"Dec 31 2029\",\"if_observed_implies\":\"Kills my central claim that the cold leg is mismeasured and the hot legs persist. My CQ2 conjunct A falls from 0.72 to ~0.45 and CQ2 drops from 35 to ~22; CQ6-C falls from 26 to ~15, CQ6-D rises from 10 to ~18, CQ6-B absorbs the rest (~43). Hypothesis plank (c) rises from 32 to ~60, i.e. all the way back to the Wave-1 centre. Does not move CQ3 or CQ6-A.\"} | {\"signpost\":\"BLS and Eurostat published indices for information, professional/technical and financial services show OUTRIGHT annual price declines of 1.0% or more for two consecutive years, in series that are market-priced rather than cost- or asset-fee-imputed (specifically: BLS PPI for software publishers and for computer systems design, and the HICP COICOP 08.3 / professional-services sub-indices).\",\"by_when\":\"Dec 31 2031\",\"if_observed_implies\":\"Directly falsifies my strongest attack on the Wave-1 panel — that the deflatable slice is only 0.08-0.12 of core and partly counter-cyclically hot. The cold leg is then real at a 0.20-0.25 weight and I lose ~0.30 pp off my core path. CQ6-C falls from 26 to ~17, CQ6-B rises to ~46, CQ2 falls to ~26, and the Wave-1 'extreme skew, dull aggregate' thesis is vindicated against me.\"} | {\"signpost\":\"IMF WEO (any vintage) or the OECD Economic Outlook publishes a medium-term China real GDP growth path for 2032-2036 averaging below 2.6%/yr, or China's realized PPP-weighted real growth for calendar 2030 and 2031 both print below 3.0%, with the property-price index still falling year-on-year.\",\"by_when\":\"Dec 31 2032\",\"if_observed_implies\":\"My central correction to Wave 1's aging-drag double-count was wrong in the direction that mattered most: the subtraction was not a double-count but a forecast of a China that does keep decelerating. CQ3 P50 falls from 2.1 to ~1.75 and P90 from 3.1 to ~2.8; CQ6-A falls from 25 to ~15, CQ6-D rises from 10 to ~17, and P2's 1.6 P50 becomes the better number. Also lowers my CQ4 P50 by ~0.15 through the (r-g) identity.\"} | {\"signpost\":\"Eurostat Structure of Earnings Survey (2030 reference year, published ~2032) plus national equivalents show ISIC Q median gross hourly earnings growing more slowly in real terms than ISIC J+K+M over 2026-2030, in a majority of reporting OECD-38 members, with no binding statutory care-pay award enacted in Germany, Japan, the UK or France in that period.\",\"by_when\":\"Dec 31 2032\",\"if_observed_implies\":\"Kills the administered-wage-jump mechanism that is the entire reason my CQ1 is 20 rather than 11. CQ1 conjunct B falls from 0.28 to ~0.12 and CQ1 drops from 20 to ~10, i.e. to the Wave-1 centre. Hypothesis plank (a) falls from 7 to ~4. Mildly lowers my OECD core path (care is 0.15 of core at 3.6% in my build), taking CQ2 to ~31 and CQ6-C to ~23.\"} | {\"signpost\":\"Strict operational OECD-38 + China data-centre electricity consumption, on the IEA's own reporting basis, is at or below 650 TWh for calendar 2032 (against ~365 TWh in 2024), OR announced hyperscaler capacity is written down by 20%+ AND measured operational TWh falls year-on-year in any year 2029-2033.\",\"by_when\":\"Dec 31 2033\",\"if_observed_implies\":\"Falsifies my 